<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\part</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{The Foundations of Autogeneration}</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(106, 153, 85);">%======================================================================</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(106, 153, 85);">%  SECTION 1</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(106, 153, 85);">%======================================================================</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\section</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{Introduction and Motivation}</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(197, 134, 192);">\label</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{</span><span style="color: rgb(156, 220, 254);">sec:intro</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">}</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">Language, cognition, and natural computation exhibit an intrinsic property of </span><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\emph</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230); font-style: italic;">autogeneration</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">}: each moment of activity encodes the conditions for its own continuation.  </span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">The concept, articulated by Barenholtz~(2025), redefines autoregression as more than a statistical convenience; it is the very mode of natural self-propagation.  </span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">In contrast to Markovian processes, where the next state depends only on the current one, autogenerative systems draw upon the entire trajectory of prior states.  </span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">They are inherently </span><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\emph</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230); font-style: italic;">non-Markovian</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">}, operating on the accumulated curvature of experience rather than on instantaneous state vectors.</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">This view renders the corpus of language not merely as data but as a </span><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\emph</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230); font-style: italic;">field of continuation rules</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">}â€”a statistical manifold whose internal topology determines its own dynamics.  </span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">In such a manifold, the ``pregnant present'' is a boundary condition that enfolds its history and projects its own future trajectory.  </span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">Autogeneration therefore serves as the bridge between memory, inference, and meaning.  </span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">The goal of Part I is to formalize that bridge using information geometry, category theory, and dynamical-systems analysis.</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
