\begin{abstract}
\thispagestyle{empty}
\vspace{2em}
{\large\bfseries How can understanding itselfâ€”an act of coherence across time, sense, and scaleâ€”be described by a single physical and mathematical law?}
&nbsp;
Current models divide explanation among thermodynamics, information theory, and computation, yet none specify why systems capable of prediction and meaning stabilize as they do.
This work proposes that cognition, physics, and inference are all manifestations of a single principle: <span>**autogeneration**</span>â€”the self-maintenance of informational curvature within a cohesive medium.
&nbsp;
Beginning from the question of how systems learn without external supervision, <span>**Part I**</span> derives a thermodynamic and geometric account of cognition as a non-Markovian flow whose equilibrium defines comprehension.
<span>**Part II**</span> reformulates this process categorically, showing that learning corresponds to adjoint functorial transformations and renormalization across interpretive scales.
<span>**Part III**</span> integrates quantum, differential, and topological layers, culminating in a <span>**cohesive ( \infty )-topos of cognition**</span> governed by an idempotent autogenerative functor ( \mathfrak{A} ).
&nbsp;
The resulting <span>**Unified Conjecture**</span> claims that every process of comprehensionâ€”neural, symbolic, or cosmologicalâ€”is an instance of this curvature-preserving closure: <span>**to know is to remain self-consistent under transformation**</span>.
&nbsp;
\vspace{2em}
\raggedleft\itshape
Understanding is the unique fixed point of reality.
\end{abstract}
